{
 "ep": 17,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Schemas and Search Path",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Why did PostgreSQL find the wrong table?",
   "cap": "Why did PostgreSQL find the *wrong table?*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode seventeen of the PostgreSQL DBA series: schemas, the search path, and qualified names.",
   "cap": "Episode 17: *schemas* and the *search path.*",
   "cue": "schemas"
  },
  {
   "say": "A database contains schemas, and schemas contain tables, views, functions and other named objects.",
   "cap": "A *database* holds *schemas.* Schemas hold *objects.*",
   "cue": "schemas"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs compare schemas to directories, except that schemas cannot be nested.",
   "cap": "Like *directories,* but *no nesting.*",
   "cue": "directories"
  },
  {
   "say": "The same name can live in different schemas. public dot orders and sales dot orders are two separate tables.",
   "cap": "*public.orders* and *sales.orders* are *different* tables.",
   "cue": "separate"
  },
  {
   "say": "Every new database has a schema called public, and by default, new tables usually land there.",
   "cap": "Every new database has a *public* schema.",
   "cue": "public"
  },
  {
   "say": "Type just orders, and PostgreSQL walks the search path, a list of schemas, taking the first match.",
   "cap": "*search_path:* a list. The *first match* wins.",
   "cue": "first"
  },
  {
   "say": "The default is dollar user, then public. A schema named after your role comes first, if it exists.",
   "cap": "Default: *\"$user\", public.* Missing ones are *skipped.*",
   "cue": "exists"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_catalog is always searched too, and if the path does not list it, it goes first.",
   "cap": "*pg_catalog* is *always* searched, first by default.",
   "cue": "always"
  },
  {
   "say": "A real test: orders exists in public and in sales. With the default path, we read the public copy.",
   "cap": "Default path: *orders* means *public.orders.*",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "Set the search path to sales, then public, and the same query reads the sales copy.",
   "cap": "Same query, now it reads *sales.orders.*",
   "cue": "sales"
  },
  {
   "say": "Same SQL, different table. Change the path in a role or database setting, and unqualified names quietly change meaning.",
   "cap": "Same SQL, *different table.* Nothing warns you.",
   "cue": "quietly"
  },
  {
   "say": "To see the resolved path, call current_schemas with true. Here: pg_catalog, sales, public.",
   "cap": "*current_schemas(true)* shows the *resolved* path.",
   "cue": "resolved"
  },
  {
   "say": "If no schema in the path has the table, you get relation does not exist, even if another schema has it.",
   "cap": "Not on the path? *relation does not exist.*",
   "cue": "exist"
  },
  {
   "say": "And with an empty search path, CREATE TABLE fails with: no schema has been selected to create in.",
   "cap": "Empty path: *no schema* has been selected.",
   "cue": "empty"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now security. A schema on your search path is a schema you trust.",
   "cap": "A schema on your path is a schema you *trust.*",
   "cue": "trust"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs warn: anyone who can create objects there can take control, and run functions as though you ran them.",
   "cap": "A malicious user can *take control* of your query.",
   "cue": "control"
  },
  {
   "say": "Since PostgreSQL fifteen, a fresh cluster stops everyone creating in public. Our test user got: permission denied.",
   "cap": "Since 15: *permission denied* to create in *public.*",
   "cue": "denied"
  },
  {
   "say": "Upgraded from fourteen or older? The old grant may remain. The fix: revoke create on schema public from public.",
   "cap": "Upgraded from 14? *REVOKE CREATE* on public.",
   "cue": "fix"
  },
  {
   "say": "Reading needs a privilege too. Without usage on a schema, you get: permission denied for schema sales.",
   "cap": "No *USAGE* on a schema? *No access.*",
   "cue": "usage"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway, a common practice: qualify names in scripts, and keep untrusted writable schemas off the path.",
   "cap": "*Qualify* names. Keep *writable* schemas off the path.",
   "cue": "qualify"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: schemas are namespaces. The search path picks the first match, and pg_catalog is always searched.",
   "cap": "Recap: *namespaces,* *first match,* *pg_catalog.*",
   "cue": "namespaces"
  },
  {
   "say": "Qualified names are explicit, and the path is a trust list.",
   "cap": "*Qualify* names. The path is a *trust list.*",
   "cue": "trust"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: roles and users in PostgreSQL. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *roles vs users.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
